/// A person's recorded decision **not** to watch a task.
///
/// Deliberately a second table rather than a column on TaskSubscription, because the two rows say
/// different things and only one of them is a delivery list. `TaskSubscription` keeps the property
/// every reader already relies on — **a row here means notify this person** — so a future fan-out
/// written the obvious way stays correct without knowing this table exists. A `mutedAt` column on
/// TaskSubscription would have inverted that silently: presence would no longer mean delivery, and
/// the one reader who forgot the predicate would notify exactly the people who asked not to be.
///
/// Only the *write* side consults this table: `subscribeUsersToTask` skips a muted user, which is
/// what makes an unsubscribe survive the next mention or assignment. Forgetting that check
/// re-subscribes somebody, which they can see and undo; forgetting a read-side predicate would
/// notify somebody who muted, which they cannot.
///
/// After this table exists, absence still means what it always meant: **no row in either table is
/// "nobody has decided anything"** — the default, which auto-subscribe is free to fill. A row here
/// is a decision, and it outranks the automatic one.
///
/// The pair (subscription AND mute for one person on one task) is contradictory and unreachable
/// through the API: both writes are transactional and each clears the other. Postgres cannot CHECK
/// across tables, so a direct Prisma write could still create one — the same app-layer-only limit
/// #36 accepted for the agent operator rule.
model TaskMute {
  id          String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  workspaceId String   @db.Uuid
  taskId      String   @db.Uuid
  userId      String   @db.Uuid
  createdAt   DateTime @default(now())

  workspace Workspace @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  task      Task      @relation(fields: [taskId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  user      User      @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([taskId, userId])
  @@index([workspaceId, userId, createdAt])
  @@index([taskId])
}

